from common import emit
rows=[
(1,'Choose a form that fits the person', ['Use frequent stem-changing verbs in a routine.','Distinguish yo-only irregularity from a stem change.','Ask a follow-up about a person’s schedule.'],[
('Patterns with limits','Present-tense stem changes normally affect stressed stems, excluding nosotros and vosotros: pienso, piensas, piensa, pensamos, pensáis, piensan. With volver, o changes to ue: vuelvo but volvemos. With pedir, e changes to i: pido but pedimos. Not every e or o changes; learn a verb’s pattern with its infinitive. Yo-only changes include hago, pongo, salgo, conozco and sé. Some verbs combine irregularities: tengo but tienes; digo but dices. Do not infer nosotros vuelvemos from vuelvo. Build a short subject-and-action phrase before adding details. You can recognize vosotros even when your normal plural address is ustedes.'),
('Two kinds of knowing','Saber commonly expresses facts or knowing how: Sé la respuesta; sé nadar. Conocer commonly expresses acquaintance with people or places: Conozco a Elena; conozco Quito. The personal a usually marks a specific human direct object: Visito a mi hermana, but visito el museo. It does not turn that person into an indirect object. Ask ¿Sabes dónde está? for information, and ¿Conoces a la profesora? for acquaintance. A useful conversation follows the answer with a relevant detail: ¿A qué hora vuelves? Avoid reciting a paradigm when your partner needs a time or a reason.')],
'Lucía trabaja en una librería. Abre a las nueve, pero ella llega antes porque quiere preparar los pedidos. Los lunes vuelve a casa a las seis. Su colega Andrés pide ayuda con un cliente: «No conozco esta novela. ¿Sabes quién la escribió?». Lucía responde: «Sé el nombre de la autora, pero no conozco su obra completa». Después hablan del horario. «¿Podemos salir temprano el viernes?», pregunta Andrés. Lucía piensa un momento: «Podemos, si terminamos los pedidos». Identifica una forma con cambio de raíz, una forma irregular solo en yo y una distinción entre saber y conocer.',
[('Complete: Nosotros ___ temprano. (volver)','volvemos','Nosotros retains the unstressed o; vuelvemos incorrectly extends the stem change.'),('Complete: Yo ___ la respuesta. (saber)','sé','The yo form sé is irregular and keeps its accent; conozco would suggest acquaintance.'),('Complete: Ella ___ ayuda. (pedir)','pide','Pedir changes e to i in this stressed singular stem.'),('Complete: Yo ___ a Elena. (conocer)','conozco','Conocer uses conozco for yo; personal a introduces the known person.'),('Complete: Ellos ___ salir. (querer)','quieren','The stressed stem changes e to ie; the second verb stays infinitive.'),('Complete: Visito ___ mi hermana.','a','A specific person as direct object normally takes personal a.')],
'Pienso en el horario|I think about the schedule;Volvemos a las seis|We return at six;Pido ayuda|I ask for help;Sé nadar|I know how to swim;Conozco a Elena|I know Elena;No sé la respuesta|I do not know the answer;Podemos salir|We can leave;Quieren trabajar|They want to work;Hago los pedidos|I prepare the orders;Pongo los libros aquí|I put the books here;Salgo temprano|I leave early;Digo la verdad|I tell the truth;¿A qué hora vuelves?|What time do you return?;Visito a mi hermana|I visit my sister;Conocemos el barrio|We know the neighborhood',
'Compare two work or study schedules. Ask three genuine follow-ups and agree when you can meet. Include one fact you know and one person or place you know.','Voseo patterns differ by region: forms such as pensás and volvés are ordinary local usage. Do not assume the stressed tú diphthong appears in a voseo form. Follow the community’s paradigm consistently.','sd-irregular'),
(2,'Routines with other people',['Use reflexive pronouns with routines and infinitives.','Distinguish an action on oneself from an action on another person.','Negotiate a practical shared routine.'],[
('Who receives the action?','Me lavo describes washing myself; lavo los platos describes washing dishes. With a body part, Spanish commonly uses the definite article: Me lavo las manos, not a required possessive equivalent of my. Reflexive pronouns match the subject: me, te, se, nos, os, se. Place one before a conjugated verb or attach it to an infinitive: Me voy a levantar / Voy a levantarme. Both positions work in this construction; do not use both at once. A stem-changing reflexive verb keeps its verb pattern: me acuesto, nos acostamos. Not every se is reflexive; later courses distinguish other uses.'),
('Routines are negotiable','Use primero, después, antes de and después de to organize a routine. After a preposition, a verb normally stays infinitive: antes de salir, después de ducharse. If a different subject follows, a finite clause is often needed; do not force every English before-sentence into an infinitive. In this unit keep the subject constant. To negotiate, state a need, ask about the other person’s need, and propose a sequence: Necesito ducharme antes de salir. ¿Te parece bien? This is more useful than listing activities without acknowledging a shared bathroom, transport schedule or caregiving responsibility.')],
'—Me levanto a las seis y media. ¿Y tú?\n—Normalmente a las siete, pero mañana necesito salir temprano.\n—Entonces puedes ducharte primero. Yo preparo el desayuno.\n—Gracias. Después de ducharme, voy a vestirme y preparar la mochila.\n—¿Te acuestas temprano esta noche?\n—Sí. Me acuesto a las diez. ¿Te parece bien usar el baño hasta las siete?\n—Sí, pero necesito lavarme las manos antes de cocinar.\nResume el acuerdo: quién usa el baño, qué hace la otra persona y qué pequeña excepción necesitan prever.',
[('Complete: Me lavo ___ manos.','las','The reflexive construction normally uses the definite article with the body part.'),('Complete: Nosotros nos ___ temprano. (acostar)','acostamos','Nosotros retains o; the reflexive pronoun does not create a stem change.'),('Complete: Voy a ___ (ducharse, yo).','ducharme','Attach the matching me pronoun to the infinitive.'),('Complete: Después de ___, desayuno. (levantarse, yo)','levantarme','A preposition takes an infinitive here; the same person performs both actions.'),('Complete: Ella ___ lava las manos.','se','The third-person subject requires the reflexive pronoun se.'),('Complete: ___ los platos. (lavar, yo)','Lavo','Washing dishes is not an action performed on oneself; no reflexive pronoun is needed.')],
'Me lavo las manos|I wash my hands;Nos acostamos temprano|We go to bed early;Voy a ducharme|I am going to shower;Me voy a duchar|I am going to shower (pronoun before verb);Antes de salir|Before leaving;Después de vestirme|After getting dressed;¿Te parece bien?|Does that work for you?;Necesito usar el baño|I need to use the bathroom;Puedes ducharte primero|You can shower first;Preparo el desayuno|I prepare breakfast;Me acuesto a las diez|I go to bed at ten;Se levanta temprano|She or he gets up early;Lavo los platos|I wash the dishes;Nos vemos a las siete|We will see each other at seven;Tenemos que organizarnos|We have to organize ourselves',
'Plan a shared morning with a partner. Each person has one non-negotiable need; find a workable order and confirm it. Solo: create two schedules and explain how you resolved a conflict.','Daily routines depend on work, climate, family and personal circumstances. Avoid treating a textbook’s timetable as a national norm. Ask individuals about their needs rather than predicting them from nationality.','sd-reflexive'),
(3,'Refer without repeating',['Identify direct objects in familiar sentences.','Choose and place lo, la, los and las.','Clarify a pronoun’s referent in a transaction.'],[
('Find what the verb affects','A direct object completes the action without an intervening recipient: Compro el libro → Lo compro. The pronouns lo, la, los, las agree with the grammatical gender and number of the object: las entradas → las. Me, te, nos and os can also be direct objects: ¿Me escuchas? Personal a may introduce a human direct object: Veo a Ana → La veo. It does not automatically require le. Keep track of the noun before replacing it. If two possible referents compete, repeat the noun instead of making the listener guess.'),
('Position and interpretation','Place the pronoun before a conjugated verb: No la necesito. With an appropriate verb-plus-infinitive construction, either before the first verb or attached to the infinitive works: Lo quiero comprar / Quiero comprarlo. Never insert it between the preposition and infinitive in voy a comprarlo. Attached pronouns are one written word. Do not replace an entire sentence with a gendered noun pronoun without considering meaning; neutral lo can refer to an idea, as in no lo sé, a use you will develop later. Some regions use le for certain male human direct objects; recognize this documented variation while practicing the course’s lo/la system.')],
'—¿Tienes las entradas para el concierto?\n—Sí, las tengo en el teléfono.\n—Perfecto. ¿Necesitamos imprimirlas?\n—No, podemos mostrarlas en la puerta.\n—¿Y el mapa? No lo encuentro.\n—Lo voy a enviar ahora.\n—Gracias. Veo a Ana en la estación. ¿La esperamos aquí?\n—Sí, pero explica dónde estamos: hay dos puertas.\nEncuentra el referente de las, lo y la. Después vuelve a contar la situación usando los sustantivos completos. Observa cuándo esa repetición ayuda y cuándo resulta innecesaria.',
[('Replace el libro: Compro el libro → ___ compro.','Lo','Libro is masculine singular and directly affected by comprar.'),('Replace las entradas: Tengo las entradas → ___ tengo.','Las','The pronoun agrees with the feminine plural noun, not the speaker.'),('Replace Ana: Veo a Ana → ___ veo.','La','Ana is a direct object despite personal a; the default pattern here uses la.'),('Attach lo to comprar.','comprarlo','An attached infinitive pronoun forms one word; do not write comprar lo.'),('Negate “La necesito.”','No la necesito','No comes before the pronoun and conjugated verb.'),('Complete: Quiero ___ las entradas. (imprimir with no pronoun)','imprimir','The second verb remains infinitive; the object is still explicitly named.')],
'Lo compro|I buy it (masculine);La necesito|I need it (feminine);Las tengo|I have them (feminine);Los veo|I see them (masculine);Quiero comprarlo|I want to buy it;Lo quiero comprar|I want to buy it (pronoun before verb);No la necesito|I do not need it (feminine);¿Me escuchas?|Can you hear me?;Podemos mostrarlas|We can show them (feminine);No lo encuentro|I cannot find it;La esperamos aquí|We wait for her here;Voy a enviarlo|I am going to send it;¿Dónde las pongo?|Where do I put them (feminine)?;Necesito el mapa|I need the map;Prefiero repetir el nombre|I prefer to repeat the name',
'Arrange tickets, a map and a meeting place. Use object pronouns, then deliberately ask which object your partner means when a reference is ambiguous.','Leísmo varies by place and referent; some uses are accepted in standard descriptions, especially masculine singular persons. Recognition of variation does not mean every pronoun is interchangeable in every context.','sd-direct'),
(4,'Give, send, and explain',['Distinguish recipient from direct object.','Combine indirect and direct pronouns in the correct order.','Confirm who receives an item or message.'],[
('The recipient and the thing','In Le doy el libro a Ana, el libro is what is given and Ana is the recipient. Indirect pronouns are me, te, le, nos, os, les. Le and les do not encode gender; number follows the recipient. A clarifying a phrase can coexist with the pronoun: Les escribo a mis colegas. Do not choose les merely because the thing sent is plural. In Les envío una carta, there is one letter but multiple recipients. Gustar already gave you experience with an indirect experiencer; now extend pronouns to transferring things and information.'),
('Two pronouns, one sequence','Put the indirect pronoun first: Te lo doy, not lo te doy. Le or les changes to se immediately before lo, la, los or las: Se lo doy a Ana; Se lo doy a mis colegas. Se here does not mean the action is reflexive, and it does not reveal the number of recipients. Clarify when necessary. In an infinitive construction, keep the pair together: Voy a dártelo / Te lo voy a dar. An attached pair may require an accent to preserve stress. Check dártelo rather than dropping the accent. In this unit, prioritize identifying the two roles before accelerating speech.')],
'—¿Le enviaste el horario a Marta?\n—Todavía no. Se lo voy a enviar ahora.\n—Envíalo también a los voluntarios.\n—Sí, les envío una copia. ¿Quieres el documento original?\n—Sí, por favor. ¿Me lo puedes dar después de la reunión?\n—Claro. Te lo doy a las cinco.\nLa primera pregunta contiene una forma de pasado que estudiarás pronto: enviaste, “you sent”. No necesitas producirla todavía. Identifica los destinatarios y el documento en cada respuesta; explica por qué se lo no permite saber por sí solo si hay uno o varios destinatarios.',
[('Complete: ___ doy el libro a Ana.','Le','Ana is one recipient; the indirect pronoun is le.'),('Replace both objects: Doy el libro a Ana → ___ doy a Ana.','Se lo','Le becomes se before masculine singular direct-object lo.'),('Complete: ___ envío una carta a mis colegas.','Les','The plural recipients require les even though carta is singular.'),('Complete: ___ lo doy a ti.','Te','Indirect te comes before direct lo; the a phrase clarifies the recipient.'),('Attach te + lo to dar, preserving stress.','dártelo','The pair stays together and the written accent preserves stress on dar.'),('Complete: No ___ lo doy a Ana.','se','Negation precedes the pronoun pair; le changes to se before lo.')],
'Le doy el libro a Ana|I give Ana the book;Se lo doy a Ana|I give it to Ana;Les envío una carta|I send them a letter;Te lo doy|I give it to you;¿Me lo puedes dar?|Can you give it to me?;Voy a dártelo|I am going to give it to you;Se la explico|I explain it to him/her/them (feminine object);Nos lo muestran|They show it to us;Te las envío|I send them to you (feminine);¿A quién se lo das?|To whom are you giving it?;Todavía no|Not yet;Después de la reunión|After the meeting;Una copia para cada persona|A copy for each person;Le escribo a Marta|I write to Marta;Les explico el plan|I explain the plan to them',
'Organize distribution of documents to three people. Change one recipient mid-conversation. Confirm the item and recipient before completing each transfer.','Pronoun doubling has regional patterns beyond this introductory lesson. A clarifying a phrase is often useful and natural; do not remove it automatically as “redundant” when it prevents misunderstanding.','sd-combined'),
(5,'Choose with reasons',['Compare qualities and quantities accurately.','Use irregular comparative forms.','Recommend an option using two relevant criteria.'],[
('Comparisons name the criterion','Use más/menos + adjective + que: Este tren es más rápido que el autobús. Equality uses tan + adjective + como: Es tan cómodo como el otro. Quantities use tanto/tanta/tantos/tantas + noun + como: Tengo tantas preguntas como tú. Match tanto to the counted noun. With a verb, use tanto como without adjective agreement: Trabajo tanto como ella. A comparison is useful only when the criterion matters: barato describes price; económico can have a wider economical sense. Clarify whether you mean total cost, travel time, accessibility or comfort.'),
('Avoid mechanical more','Common irregular comparisons include mejor, peor, mayor and menor. Mejor is often better, not más bueno in the basic comparison of quality; mayor commonly compares age. Más grande remains useful for physical size, and context changes the choice. A relative superlative uses an article: Es la opción más barata de las tres. Before a number threshold, more than usually uses de: más de veinte euros. Contrast with a direct comparison: gasto más que tú. Explain one disadvantage as well as one advantage; a balanced recommendation helps the listener choose under their own priorities.')],
'Lucía y Andrés comparan tres rutas. El tren cuesta veinte euros y tarda cuarenta minutos. El autobús cuesta doce euros, pero tarda una hora y diez. La bicicleta no tiene ese costo, aunque requiere más esfuerzo y depende del tiempo. Lucía prefiere el tren porque hoy tiene una cita importante. Andrés elige el autobús: tiene más tiempo que Lucía y quiere gastar menos de quince euros. Ninguna opción es “la mejor” sin conocer las necesidades. Explica qué criterio usa cada persona y recomienda una ruta para alguien con poco tiempo y un presupuesto de veinticinco euros.',
[('Complete: Es más rápido ___ el autobús.','que','Que introduces the other member of a comparison.'),('Complete: Es tan cómodo ___ el tren.','como','Equality pairs tan with como, not que.'),('Complete: Tengo ___ preguntas como tú.','tantas','Preguntas is feminine plural, so the quantity word agrees.'),('Complete: Cuesta más ___ veinte euros.','de','A numeric threshold normally takes de in this construction.'),('Supply the basic comparative of bueno for quality.','mejor','Mejor is the usual irregular comparative of quality; avoid mechanically adding más.'),('Complete: Es ___ opción más barata.','la','The feminine noun opción requires la in this relative superlative.')],
'Más rápido que el autobús|Faster than the bus;Tan cómodo como el tren|As comfortable as the train;Tantas preguntas como tú|As many questions as you;Más de veinte euros|More than twenty euros;La opción más barata|The cheapest option;Es mejor para mí|It is better for me;Tengo menos tiempo|I have less time;Cuesta menos de quince euros|It costs less than fifteen euros;Depende de tus prioridades|It depends on your priorities;Trabajo tanto como ella|I work as much as she does;Es mayor que yo|She or he is older than I am;Es más grande|It is bigger;Es peor para este caso|It is worse for this case;Aunque cuesta más|Although it costs more;Prefiero esta opción porque…|I prefer this option because…',
'Choose transport or accommodation with a partner who values a different criterion. Compare three options, state the tradeoff, and agree on a choice.','Price comparisons should use the actual currency and local costs; the fictional prices here do not describe a real destination. Accessibility, safety and caregiving needs can matter more than the cheapest fare.','sd-comparisons'),
(6,'Tell what happened',['Form regular preterite verbs with accurate accents.','Present a bounded past event in sequence.','Complete a cumulative information exchange without notes.'],[
('A completed event viewpoint','The preterite presents an event as a bounded whole: Ayer compré un libro. Regular ar endings are é, aste, ó, amos, asteis, aron; er/ir endings are í, iste, ió, imos, isteis, ieron. The first and third singular accents distinguish forms from present-tense words: hablo versus habló. Nosotros hablamos may be present or preterite; context supplies the time. A time phrase does not mechanically dictate a tense, but ayer and el sábado pasado help establish a completed episode. Say who did what and what followed before adding background detail, which comes with the imperfect.'),
('Sequence and cumulative use','Some spelling changes preserve a sound in the yo preterite: buscar → busqué, llegar → llegué, empezar → empecé. They do not mean the same spelling change appears in every person. Negative preterite sentences still put no before the verb: No compré las entradas. Add pronouns when the referent is clear: Las compré ayer. For the midterm, combine a routine, a comparison, a recipient and a completed action. A quiz checks controlled choices; the conversation and writing sample separately show whether you can integrate them. Preserve your first attempt and explain corrections rather than repeatedly guessing.')],
'Ayer Lucía terminó el trabajo a las cinco. Caminó hasta la estación y compró dos entradas. Después llamó a Andrés: «Las compré para la función de las siete. Te envío la dirección». Andrés contestó: «Perfecto. Ayer gasté demasiado, pero hoy puedo pagar mi entrada». Llegaron temprano y hablaron en la plaza. Al final, Lucía escribió una nota para recordar el título de la obra. Ordena los acontecimientos sin mirar el texto; después cuenta la historia como si fueras Lucía, cambiando las formas necesarias.',
[('Complete: Ayer yo ___ un libro. (comprar)','compré','The regular yo preterite ends in é; the accent distinguishes it from other forms.'),('Complete: Ella ___ el trabajo. (terminar, preterite)','terminó','Third-person singular ar preterite ends in ó, not present o.'),('Complete: Ustedes ___ la dirección. (escribir, preterite)','escribieron','Regular ir preterite takes ieron in third-person plural.'),('Complete: Yo ___ las entradas. (buscar, preterite)','busqué','Qu preserves the hard c sound before the é ending.'),('Complete: Yo ___ temprano. (llegar, preterite)','llegué','Gu preserves the hard g sound before é.'),('Complete: Yo ___ a las ocho. (empezar, preterite)','empecé','Final z changes to c before the yo preterite é ending.')],
'Ayer compré las entradas|Yesterday I bought the tickets;Terminó a las cinco|She or he finished at five;Escribieron la dirección|They wrote the address;Busqué el documento|I looked for the document;Llegué temprano|I arrived early;Empecé a las ocho|I started at eight;Las compré ayer|I bought them yesterday (feminine);No gasté mucho|I did not spend much;El sábado pasado|Last Saturday;Después llamé a Andrés|Afterward I called Andrés;Hablamos en la plaza|We talked in the square;¿Cuándo terminaste?|When did you finish?;Primero caminamos|First we walked;Al final escribí una nota|At the end I wrote a note;Me explicó el problema|She or he explained the problem to me',
'Midterm: negotiate an appointment, compare two plans, explain who receives the information, and recount yesterday’s preparation. Write 120–160 words and answer three follow-up questions.','Narratives may use the historical present for vividness, but practice establishing a clear past frame here. A regional storytelling habit is not a reason to ignore the listener’s need for a coherent timeline.','sd-preterite'),
(7,'When the past changes its stem',['Use frequent irregular preterite stems.','Distinguish ir and ser through context.','Clarify a sequence when an event is unexpected.'],[
('Strong preterite stems','Common irregular stems include tuv-, estuv-, pud-, pus-, sup-, quis-, vin-, hic- and dij-. Most use e, iste, o, imos, isteis, ieron without the regular singular accents: tuve, tuvo, tuvimos. Hacer has hizo in the third singular, preserving pronunciation. J stems use eron in the third plural: dijeron, trajeron, not dijieron. These forms must be learned with examples, not produced by applying a present stem change. Tener → tuve is not tiení. Group verbs by useful events: tuve un problema, pude entrar, puse la dirección, vino una amiga.'),
('Meaning still comes from context','Ir and ser share fui, fuiste, fue, fuimos, fuisteis, fueron. Fuimos al museo indicates going; fue una experiencia interesante identifies the experience. Dar and ver are short irregulars: di, diste, dio; vi, viste, vio. They do not take the imagined accents dió or vió. Some verbs express a change or bounded achievement in preterite contexts: supe la noticia can mean I found out; pude entrar can mean I managed to enter. These are contextual readings, not a claim that each tense translates to one fixed English verb. Explain the event and result so the intended meaning is clear.')],
'El sábado fuimos al museo, pero tuvimos un problema en la entrada. Andrés puso una fecha incorrecta en la reserva. La empleada vio el mensaje y dijo: «Podemos cambiarla». Nos dio dos entradas nuevas. Al final pudimos entrar. La visita fue interesante: vimos una exposición sobre el barrio y supimos por qué cambió su nombre. Lucía hizo varias preguntas. Al salir, Andrés dijo: «La próxima vez voy a revisar la fecha dos veces». Distingue los usos de fuimos y fue; luego identifica el problema, la ayuda y el resultado.',
[('Complete: Nosotros ___ un problema. (tener, preterite)','tuvimos','Tener uses the irregular stem tuv- and the nosotros ending imos.'),('Complete: Ella ___ una pregunta. (hacer, preterite)','hizo','Third-person singular hacer uses hizo, with z before o.'),('Complete: Ellos ___ la verdad. (decir, preterite)','dijeron','The j stem takes eron, not ieron, in third-person plural.'),('Complete: Yo ___ la noticia ayer. (saber, preterite)','supe','Saber has the irregular preterite stem sup-, often describing finding out in this context.'),('Complete: Ella me ___ una entrada. (dar, preterite)','dio','Dio is written without an accent; it is not a regular ar ending.'),('Complete: Nosotros ___ al museo. (ir, preterite)','fuimos','The destination al museo establishes ir rather than ser despite the shared forms.')],
'Tuvimos un problema|We had a problem;Hizo una pregunta|She or he asked a question;Dijeron la verdad|They told the truth;Supe la noticia|I found out the news;Me dio una entrada|She or he gave me a ticket;Fuimos al museo|We went to the museum;Fue interesante|It was interesting;Pude entrar|I managed to enter;Puso la dirección|She or he put the address;Vino una amiga|A friend came;Vi el mensaje|I saw the message;Quise ayudar|I wanted or attempted to help in that episode;Estuve allí|I was there during that episode;Nos trajeron agua|They brought us water;Al final lo resolvimos|In the end we solved it',
'Recount a reservation problem to a partner playing a helpful employee. Explain the sequence and result; the partner asks which date or person you mean.','Service encounters vary in register and procedure. A polite explanation of a mistake is more useful than assuming a worker can bend a rule. Describe what happened, ask what is possible, and listen to the answer.','sd-preterite'),
(8,'Describe what life used to be like',['Form the imperfect, including ser, ir and ver.','Describe past routines and circumstances.','Avoid turning an English translation cue into a tense rule.'],[
('A past viewed from inside','The imperfect presents a past situation without focusing on its endpoints: Vivíamos cerca del río. Regular ar endings are aba, abas, aba, ábamos, abais, aban; er/ir endings are ía, ías, ía, íamos, íais, ían. The three commonly taught irregular verbs are ser (era, eras, era, éramos, erais, eran), ir (iba…) and ver (veía…). First and third singular forms coincide, so context or a name can matter. Do not assume the imperfect means an event never ended; it simply does not present the ending as the main point.'),
('Habits and background','De niño jugaba en el parque describes a recurring activity. Eran las ocho, hacía frío, and tenía diez años often supply background time, weather and age. Those functions help explain tense choice, but the same information can be framed as a bounded event in other contexts. English used to is helpful for habits, while was/were may correspond to either Spanish past tense. Write a picture of the setting before introducing a new event. If a listener asks what happened next, an endless string of imperfect descriptions may not answer the question; Unit 9 combines the two perspectives.')],
'Cuando Lucía era niña, vivía en un barrio pequeño. Su abuela tenía una tienda y conocía a casi todos los vecinos. Los sábados Lucía iba a la plaza con sus primos. Jugaban, compraban pan y volvían antes de comer. La plaza era diferente: había menos coches y más árboles. Lucía no dice que todo fuera mejor; recuerda también que el transporte era limitado. Hoy quiere saber cómo recuerdan el barrio otras personas. Separa hechos descritos de valoraciones personales y prepara dos preguntas que no sugieran que todos tuvieron la misma experiencia.',
[('Complete: Nosotros ___ cerca. (vivir, imperfect)','vivíamos','The nosotros er/ir imperfect ending is íamos, with its written accent.'),('Complete: Ella ___ a la plaza. (ir, imperfect)','iba','Ir has the irregular imperfect stem iba; it does not follow regular ir endings.'),('Complete: Nosotros ___ estudiantes. (ser, imperfect)','éramos','Ser uses éramos, with stress preserved by the written accent.'),('Complete: Yo ___ a mi abuela. (ver, imperfect)','veía','Ver retains the e in veía; the accent marks the stressed í.'),('Complete: De niño ___ en el parque. (jugar, yo imperfect)','jugaba','Regular ar imperfect uses aba; the present stem change does not carry over.'),('Complete: ___ las ocho. (ser, imperfect)','Eran','Plural clock time uses eran in this background description.')],
'Vivíamos cerca del río|We lived near the river;Iba a la plaza|I used to go to the square;Éramos estudiantes|We were students;Veía a mi abuela|I used to see my grandmother;Jugaba en el parque|I used to play in the park;Eran las ocho|It was eight o’clock;Hacía frío|It was cold;Tenía diez años|I was ten years old;Había más árboles|There were more trees;Los sábados volvíamos temprano|On Saturdays we used to return early;Cuando era niña|When I was a girl;Antes vivía aquí|I used to live here;Conocía a los vecinos|She or he knew the neighbors;No todo era mejor|Not everything was better;¿Cómo era el barrio?|What was the neighborhood like?',
'Interview a willing person about a past routine, using fictional information if preferred. Ask about setting, habit and one difference from today; avoid assuming the past was better.','Memory is selective. A nostalgic description is one person’s perspective, not historical proof about an entire place. Compare experiences respectfully and distinguish memory from a verified public fact.','sd-imperfect'),
(9,'Make a story move',['Choose past-tense viewpoint according to narrative purpose.','Connect background and a bounded event.','Retell an event with clarification and an ending.'],[
('Background and advancing events','Mientras caminaba, vi a Ana presents walking as the ongoing background and seeing Ana as a bounded event. The distinction is viewpoint, not action length: Trabajé allí veinte años can bound a long period; estaba un momento en la puerta can describe a brief scene. Habit words do not force imperfect in every context: De niño jugaba allí describes a habit, while Durante aquel verano jugué allí todos los días bounds the series. Ask whether you are setting the scene, presenting a recurring situation, or advancing the sequence with an event.'),
('Change the viewpoint, change the message','Conocía a Ana says I already knew her; conocí a Ana often presents meeting her. Sabía la respuesta describes knowledge; supe la respuesta can present learning it. No quería salir describes a state of reluctance; no quise salir can present a bounded refusal. These are tendencies in context, not memorized one-to-one translation equations. Plan a story in three parts: setting, complication and outcome. Use a repair phrase if your listener loses the timeline: Primero…, antes de eso…, lo que pasó después fue…. Retelling from another viewpoint helps reveal what each tense contributes.')],
'Era viernes y llovía. Lucía esperaba el autobús cuando vio una cartera en el suelo. La recogió y buscó un nombre. Mientras miraba una tarjeta, llegó un hombre que parecía preocupado. «Perdí la cartera», dijo. Lucía no se la entregó inmediatamente: primero le pidió que describiera su contenido. El hombre dio los detalles correctos y ella se la devolvió. Después llegó el autobús. Lucía conocía el barrio, pero nunca había hablado con aquel vecino. Identifica el escenario, los sucesos que avanzan la historia y la razón de pedir una descripción.',
[('Complete: Mientras caminaba, ___ a Ana. (ver, bounded event)','vi','The new bounded event uses preterite vi against imperfect background caminaba.'),('Complete: ___ viernes y llovía. (ser, background)','Era','Era sets the scene without presenting an endpoint.'),('Complete: Yo ___ a Ana ayer por primera vez. (conocer)','conocí','A first meeting is presented as a bounded event with conocí.'),('Complete: Ya ___ a Ana antes de la reunión. (conocer, yo background)','conocía','The sentence describes an existing acquaintance, not the meeting event.'),('Complete: Trabajé allí veinte años. Is this period presented as bounded? Write sí or no.','sí','A long duration can still be presented as a completed whole; length does not force imperfect.'),('Complete: Mientras ella ___, llegó el autobús. (esperar, background)','esperaba','The ongoing waiting supplies background for the arrival.')],
'Mientras caminaba, vi a Ana|While I was walking, I saw Ana;Era viernes y llovía|It was Friday and it was raining;Conocí a Ana ayer|I met Ana yesterday;Ya conocía a Ana|I already knew Ana;Trabajé allí veinte años|I worked there for twenty years as a bounded period;Mientras esperaba, llegó el autobús|While I was waiting, the bus arrived;Primero recogí la cartera|First I picked up the wallet;Antes de eso|Before that;Después se la devolví|Afterward I returned it to him or her;Parecía preocupado|He seemed worried;No quería salir|I did not want to go out as a background state;No quise salir|I refused or chose not to go out in that episode;Ya sabía la respuesta|I already knew the answer;Supe la respuesta después|I found out the answer afterward;¿Qué pasó al final?|What happened in the end?',
'Tell a story with a setting, complication and outcome. Your partner interrupts twice to ask when or why. Retell it in writing and explain three tense choices.','Narrative conventions vary, but listeners everywhere benefit from clear reference and sequence. Avoid presenting your interpretation of a stranger’s motives as an established fact; use parecía or según dijo when appropriate.','sd-past-contrast'),
(10,'Give useful instructions',['Form common affirmative and negative commands.','Place object pronouns in instructions.','Adapt advice to audience and relationship.'],[
('Affirmative commands and address','A regular affirmative tú command usually matches the present él/ella form: habla, come, escribe. Frequent irregulars are di, haz, ve, pon, sal, sé, ten, ven. Do not confuse command sé (be) with se. Usted and ustedes commonly use the present yo stem with opposite endings: hablo → hable/hablen; como → coma/coman. Negative tú uses that same family with s: no hables, no comas. These forms belong to the subjunctive system developed in Course III. Spelling and irregular yo forms matter: busque, no busques; tenga, no tengas. Learn commands in context, not as permission to order strangers around.'),
('Pronouns and politeness','Attach pronouns to affirmative commands: dime, dámelo, siéntate. Put them before negative commands: no me lo des, no te sientes. Written accents may preserve stress after attachment. A polite request may use ¿Puede…? rather than a direct command; por favor helps but does not erase an inappropriate tone or demand. With a familiar partner, explain the reason for advice: Descansa un poco porque pareces cansado. Recognize vosotros affirmative infinitive-r → d (hablad) and common voseo forms such as hablá, but use the paradigm appropriate to your interaction. Avoid mixing tú and usted endings unintentionally.')],
'—Necesito llegar al centro de salud. ¿Me puede explicar el camino?\n—Claro. Siga esta calle y cruce en el semáforo. No cruce antes: hay mucho tráfico. Después gire a la derecha.\n—¿Está junto al mercado?\n—Sí. Lleve su documento, pero no me lo dé a mí; muéstrelo en recepción.\n—Gracias. ¿Puede repetir el último paso?\nLa explicación usa usted. Reescribe tres instrucciones para un amigo tratado de tú, conservando la información y el cuidado práctico. No conviertas este diálogo de orientación en asesoramiento médico.',
[('Give the affirmative tú command of hablar.','habla','Regular affirmative tú uses the third-person present form.'),('Give the negative tú command of hablar, including no.','no hables','Negative tú uses the opposite-vowel subjunctive-family form.'),('Give the usted command of tener.','tenga','The irregular yo stem teng- supplies the command form.'),('Attach me + lo to the affirmative tú command da.','dámelo','Affirmative commands attach the pair and retain stress with an accent.'),('Complete: No ___ lo des. (to me)','me','Negative commands place the pronouns before the verb.'),('Give the affirmative tú command meaning be.','sé','The command sé requires an accent and differs from the pronoun se.')],
'Habla más despacio|Speak more slowly (tú);No hables tan rápido|Do not speak so quickly (tú);Tenga cuidado|Be careful (usted);Dámelo, por favor|Give it to me, please (tú);No me lo des todavía|Do not give it to me yet;Sé paciente|Be patient (tú);Siga esta calle|Follow this street (usted);Cruce en el semáforo|Cross at the traffic light (usted);Gire a la derecha|Turn right (usted);Lleve su documento|Bring your document (usted);Muéstrelo en recepción|Show it at reception (usted);Descansa un poco|Rest a little (tú);Ven aquí, por favor|Come here, please (tú);Haz una pausa|Take a break (tú);¿Puede repetir el último paso?|Can you repeat the last step?',
'Give a partner a five-step practical procedure. Include one prohibition, explain its reason, and check understanding. Then adapt the instructions for usted.','Directness is shaped by relationship, setting and local convention. A grammatically correct command can still be socially inappropriate. Observe how people request help in the actual community rather than equating a pronoun with politeness by itself.','sd-commands'),
(11,'Purpose, cause, and experience',['Distinguish common por and para meanings in context.','Form the present perfect with haber and a participle.','Recognize regional variation in recent-past framing.'],[
('Reason and intended destination','Para commonly introduces a goal, recipient, intended use or deadline: Estudio para trabajar aquí; es para Ana; para el viernes. Por commonly introduces a reason, route, exchange, duration in some contexts or agent: Gracias por ayudar; paso por el parque; lo compré por diez euros. These are meaning families, not a complete word-for-word translation of for. Contrast Lo hice por Ana (because of/on behalf of Ana) with Lo hice para Ana (intended for Ana). Some contexts admit both with a change in perspective. Explain what you mean instead of asking which preposition is always “correct” after an English word.'),
('Experience connected to now','Present perfect uses he, has, ha, hemos, habéis, han plus a participle. Regular participles end in ado or ido: hablado, comido, vivido. Frequent irregulars include hecho, dicho, visto, escrito, puesto, vuelto, abierto and roto. With haber, the participle does not agree with the subject or object: Hemos escrito las cartas. This differs from an adjective: las cartas están escritas. Ya, todavía no and alguna vez can help discuss experience. Many Latin American speakers use the preterite where some speakers in Spain prefer the present perfect for a recent period; neither region’s distribution should be imposed universally. Learn the form and attend to local use.')],
'—¿Has preparado el informe para Ana?\n—Sí, lo he escrito, pero todavía no lo he enviado.\n—Gracias por hacerlo. Lo necesita para el viernes.\n—Hoy pasé por su oficina, pero no estaba.\n—Puedes enviárselo por correo.\n—Buena idea. ¿Alguna vez has trabajado con ella?\n—Sí, trabajé con ella el año pasado.\nExplica el propósito de para y los usos de por. Identifica las formas de haber y los participios. La alternancia entre pasé y he escrito es compatible con una conversación real; no cambies todas las formas por una supuesta regla regional absoluta.',
[('Complete: Estudio ___ trabajar aquí.','para','The infinitive expresses the intended goal of studying.'),('Complete: Gracias ___ ayudarme.','por','Por introduces the action for which thanks are given.'),('Complete: Es un regalo ___ Ana.','para','Ana is the intended recipient of the gift.'),('Complete: Hemos ___ las cartas. (escribir)','escrito','Escrito is irregular and stays invariant after haber despite plural cartas.'),('Complete: Yo ___ visto la película.','he','Yo takes he in the present perfect auxiliary paradigm.'),('Complete: Todavía no lo ___ hecho. (nosotros)','hemos','Nosotros takes hemos; the participle hecho does not change for number.')],
'Estudio para trabajar aquí|I study in order to work here;Gracias por ayudarme|Thank you for helping me;Es para Ana|It is intended for Ana;Hemos escrito las cartas|We have written the letters;He visto la película|I have seen the film;Todavía no lo hemos hecho|We have not done it yet;Para el viernes|By or for Friday as a deadline;Pasé por el parque|I passed through the park;Lo compré por diez euros|I bought it for ten euros;Lo hice por Ana|I did it because of or on behalf of Ana;Lo hice para Ana|I made it for Ana;¿Alguna vez has viajado allí?|Have you ever traveled there?;Ya he terminado|I have already finished;Lo he puesto aquí|I have put it here;Se lo he dicho|I have told it to him/her/them',
'Plan a shared project: explain its purpose, recipient and deadline, then report what you have done and what remains. Ask a partner about one relevant past experience.','Present-perfect usage differs within as well as between countries. Listen to the actual speech community. A course may teach a useful contrast without treating one variety’s recent-past pattern as a universal test of correctness.','sd-perfect'),
(12,'A journey with a story',['Integrate present, past, pronouns and practical requests.','Tell a coherent travel or local-exploration story.','Use evidence to choose the next review priorities.'],[
('A project with a real listener','A useful travel account answers where, why, what happened and what the listener needs to know. Begin with a goal, give enough setting to orient the listener, select two or three events, and end with a result or recommendation. Use imperfect for background where appropriate and preterite to bound events; do not alternate mechanically. Replace repeated objects only when the referent stays clear. A listener unfamiliar with the place may need a map, an explained local term or a repeated name. Keep personal safety and private information in mind: invented itineraries work equally well for the language task.'),
('Review the message and the language','Before the final, produce a short account without notes. Then check direct/indirect roles, agreement, past viewpoint, and por/para meaning. Separate quiz accuracy from a writing rubric and a live interaction rubric. Your capstone includes an unexpected change: a canceled reservation, a dietary requirement or a missed connection. The aim is to respond, clarify and adapt, not to perform a flawless memorized monologue. Save the first version, feedback, and revision. If errors recur, revisit the unit that explains their function, then test the correction in a new situation rather than only retaking a familiar question.')],
'El mes pasado hice una excursión con dos amigos. Queríamos conocer un pueblo cercano porque una amiga nos había recomendado su mercado. Cuando llegamos, llovía y algunas tiendas estaban cerradas. Primero buscamos un lugar para comer. Preguntamos qué llevaba la sopa y pedimos una opción sin carne. Después visitamos una exposición. La entrada era barata y la guía nos explicó la historia del edificio. Al volver, perdimos el autobús, pero una empleada nos mostró otra ruta. No todo salió como esperábamos; aun así, aprendimos a preguntar mejor y a confirmar los horarios. ¿Qué harías tú ante un cambio de transporte? Puedes responder con un plan usando ir a.',
[('Complete: Ayer ___ una excursión. (hacer, yo)','hice','Hacer uses the irregular stem hic- in the yo preterite.'),('Complete: Cuando llegamos, ___ mucho. (llover, background)','llovía','The imperfect presents weather as the background to arrival.'),('Complete: La guía ___ explicó la historia a nosotros.','nos','Nos represents the recipients of the explanation.'),('Complete: Buscamos un lugar ___ comer.','para','Para introduces the purpose of finding the place.'),('Complete: No ___ lo dé todavía. (to me, usted command)','me','The negative command places the indirect/direct pair before the verb.'),('Complete: Ya hemos ___ el plan. (hacer)','hecho','The present perfect uses the irregular participle hecho, which remains invariant.')],
'Hice una excursión|I went on an excursion;Cuando llegamos, llovía|When we arrived, it was raining;Nos explicó la historia|She or he explained the history to us;Un lugar para comer|A place to eat;No me lo dé todavía|Do not give it to me yet (usted);Ya hemos hecho el plan|We have already made the plan;Queríamos conocer el pueblo|We wanted to get to know the town;Pedimos una opción sin carne|We ordered an option without meat;Perdimos el autobús|We missed the bus;Nos mostró otra ruta|She or he showed us another route;No todo salió como esperábamos|Not everything went as we expected;Aun así, aprendimos mucho|Even so, we learned a lot;Voy a confirmar el horario|I am going to confirm the schedule;¿Qué recomienda usted?|What do you recommend? (usted);Necesito aclarar un detalle|I need to clarify one detail',
'Final capstone: create a 180–220-word account and a three-minute travel or neighborhood presentation. Add practical advice and negotiate an unexpected change with a partner. Save a first draft, feedback and revision.','Travel learning should not reduce communities to attractions or stereotypes. Attribute recommendations, ask permission before recording people, and distinguish your short visit from expertise about a place.','sd-past-contrast')]
